WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.707 --> 00:00:15.448
Hello my name is Manu Prakash. I run a lab at Stanford and today I want to tell you about

00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:22.889
a new project in our lab that we call Foldscope, which is a completely functional microscope

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.893
that you can make by folding paper.

00:00:26.894 --> 00:00:27.927
Let me tell you a little bit of the background

00:00:27.928 --> 00:00:35.935
about my lab. I work in the field of organismic biophysics, so I apply the ideas of soft condensed matter physics

00:00:35.936 --> 00:00:41.207
to the complexity and biodiversity that we see on our planet. We primarily

00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:50.082
work on basal metazoans, where we see all kinds of complexity in life forms that evolve on this planet.

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:56.322
And, I'm just amazed by the microcosmos that exist on this planet

00:00:56.323 --> 00:01:02.595
that we just live and breathe every single day. But there are days where I'm actually jealous

00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:10.803
of my astronomy colleagues. And the reason I say that is that you can find a dark spot, and look up on the night sky

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:17.009
and just with unaided eyes, try to comprehend the vastness

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.480
of the universe that we live in. And every single person on this planet

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:26.018
can have this experience very, very quickly.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:30.957
But if you wanted to do that for microscopy, or the microcosmos, it is

00:01:30.958 --> 00:01:35.061
an extremely difficult challenge. So, several years ago

00:01:35.062 --> 00:01:39.832
when I started my lab, I divided my attention to another field

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:44.237
that we call frugal science, where the goal is to build scientific

00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:49.208
high end instruments that are available to every single person on

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:53.746
this planet. We call this field frugal science primarily

00:01:53.747 --> 00:01:57.650
because the tools that we develop are trying to democratize

00:01:57.651 --> 00:02:01.420
the access of science to every single person.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:05.591
The inspiration for this started for me when I

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:09.428
visited Ghandi's Ashram, Sabarmati server years ago.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:12.698
It was quite an accidental visit that I stumbled upon this

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:18.137
picture where Ghandi is essentially either looking at a slide for

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:22.842
leprosy or malaria. I find this image

00:02:22.843 --> 00:02:27.079
fascinating, because in one single image, it captures how science

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.082
and society connects together, and how you can

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:33.152
actually use science to better humanity.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:36.189
One of the goals that microscopy is used for is

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.592
field diagnostics, especially for infectious diseases.

00:02:39.593 --> 00:02:44.063
and the reason for that is that most infectious diseases

00:02:44.064 --> 00:02:47.033
have faces, whether it's African sleeping sickness

00:02:47.034 --> 00:02:52.438
or Giardia or Leishmania or Malaria. They all have

00:02:52.439 --> 00:02:55.374
different forms that you can identify using microscopy.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:03:00.213
One of the other reasons that I am interested in the field of

00:03:00.214 --> 00:03:03.349
infectious diseases and global health is because I grew up

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.520
in a developing country. And, for me, while growing up

00:03:07.521 --> 00:03:13.259
I felt that the need for scientific tools and the fact that as

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.395
a kid, access to scientific tools were only

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:19.765
available to a very few set of people. So, one of the goals

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:23.035
and drives of this project is to try and change that, in a

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:28.808
little bit. This is one of the images of how

00:03:28.809 --> 00:03:32.812
society felt several centuries ago, when for the very

00:03:32.813 --> 00:03:36.549
first time, they realized that there is a microcosmic

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:41.387
life forms that all way to things that cause diseases

00:03:41.388 --> 00:03:44.390
to things that actually make us alive. Uh, here

00:03:44.391 --> 00:03:48.561
is a picture of monsters depicted in the cup soup of

00:03:48.562 --> 00:03:54.133
a British mom, a lithograph from the 1800s.

00:03:54.134 --> 00:03:57.870
One of the inspirations for our project was large scale

00:03:57.871 --> 00:04:00.940
manufacturing. So we looked at two different techniques.

00:04:00.941 --> 00:04:04.677
One, is the art of folding paper, which is called origami.

00:04:04.678 --> 00:04:09.081
Origami is fascinating, because it allows you to build complex structures

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:14.720
with a single sheet of paper by primarily folding it.

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:18.090
The other inspiration that we draw is from a visit that I had

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:22.395
some time ago to a matchstick factory, where I learned that you can build

00:04:22.396 --> 00:04:26.165
billions of matchsticks and matchboxes in less than a month

00:04:26.166 --> 00:04:30.603
primarily using roll to roll manufacturing. We utilized both these techniques

00:04:30.604 --> 00:04:35.074
to manufacture a unique kind of microscope which we call Foldscope.

00:04:35.075 --> 00:04:40.012
So here is an image or CAD rendering of what the instrument looks like.

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:44.817
I have the instrument right in my pocket. Here is an instrument that's

00:04:44.818 --> 00:04:48.254
built primarily by folding a flat sheet of paper.

00:04:48.255 --> 00:04:51.557
All the optics and electronics are printed on this

00:04:51.558 --> 00:04:54.994
sheet, and then it's folded together to make a fully functional

00:04:54.995 --> 00:04:58.965
instrument that costs us in part roughly around 1 dollar

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:02.134
to make, and we can do imaging all the way from

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:07.006
700-800nm resolution. We can get magnification all the way

00:05:07.007 --> 00:05:13.679
from 140x to 2100x. One of the fascinating things about these instruments

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.818
is the fact that it's designed completely independently. It has a tiny slot

00:05:18.819 --> 00:05:22.989
for taking traditional glass slides. The way you use them is using two hands

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:28.461
you move around for panning and the tension in your hand essentially changes your

00:05:28.462 --> 00:05:32.632
focusing. So to give you a view of what the complexity

00:05:32.633 --> 00:05:35.468
of this instrument looks like in such a simple

00:05:35.469 --> 00:05:39.105
form-factor. If I was to take this instrument and cut this

00:05:39.106 --> 00:05:42.942
in a side view, what you would see is several different

00:05:42.943 --> 00:05:45.678
components all the way from a light-emitting diode

00:05:45.679 --> 00:05:49.148
condenser lenses, several different apertures, the sample

00:05:49.149 --> 00:05:53.753
different optically lens configurations for different magnifications

00:05:53.754 --> 00:05:56.956
and all the mechanics that goes in into making a fully functional

00:05:56.957 --> 00:06:01.961
instrument. To give you a fair idea of what all the parts

00:06:01.962 --> 00:06:06.199
look like, here is a simple sketch. So what you're looking at here is

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:10.136
what we call a sample holding stage. Right here is

00:06:10.137 --> 00:06:14.841
the optics stage. And right at the bottom is an illumination stage.

00:06:14.842 --> 00:06:18.945
All the optics is printed into these tiny little modules

00:06:18.946 --> 00:06:22.048
which we use polymer optics or glass optics that's

00:06:22.049 --> 00:06:25.218
printed in these little modules that can be inserted.

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:30.122
Folding allows us to align these instruments directly into

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:35.127
one single sheet, and once the instrument is put together

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:38.798
these 3 different stages come together as a single unit.

00:06:38.799 --> 00:06:42.535
To just give you an example, here are the 3 different

00:06:42.536 --> 00:06:46.472
parts cut using a die-cutting process on a piece of paper

00:06:46.473 --> 00:06:50.476
Here is what we call as a sample holding stage,

00:06:50.477 --> 00:06:54.480
Here is the optics stage,

00:06:54.481 --> 00:06:58.451
with the optics module actually put in.

00:06:58.452 --> 00:07:01.954
And thirdly we have an illumination stage which has the light

00:07:01.955 --> 00:07:06.425
source, a watch battery, a switch, and an LED

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:09.595
with a condenser lens on the other side. Each one of these

00:07:09.596 --> 00:07:14.100
stages folds and interweaves together to form an

00:07:14.101 --> 00:07:19.105
individual microscope that looks something like this.

00:07:19.106 --> 00:07:22.208
It takes maybe of the order of 2-3 minutes to fold this

00:07:22.209 --> 00:07:27.180
object in, and folding gives you a fully functional instrument.

00:07:27.181 --> 00:07:32.518
There is a tiny little slot right here, that allows you to put a glass slide

00:07:32.519 --> 00:07:35.388
so I'm just going to do a quick test to show you

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:42.228
how to put the sample in. So, at this point

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:45.198
the sample has been registered to the slide,

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:50.269
and you can image it very directly to your eye either by looking right

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:54.507
here, or essentially using this instrument in a projection

00:07:54.508 --> 00:07:57.977
mode where the image is projected outward. The way to use

00:07:57.978 --> 00:08:01.714
the instrument is by both your hands where you move your

00:08:01.715 --> 00:08:05.518
hands to change the field of view, and the tension

00:08:05.519 --> 00:08:09.121
in your hand gives you focusing. So just slight

00:08:09.122 --> 00:08:12.158
tension in my hand allows me to move my focal

00:08:12.159 --> 00:08:16.329
plane up and down to actually focus tightly on the

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.398
sample that I care about. There is a lot of number of parts

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:23.135
that go in to allow us the precision of this folding

00:08:23.136 --> 00:08:27.974
that I mention. And each one of these instruments is then manufactured using

00:08:27.975 --> 00:08:32.211
high throughput techniques.

00:08:32.212 --> 00:08:35.414
One of the fascinations that we have about microscopy

00:08:35.415 --> 00:08:39.886
is this idea, that why not build instruments with our own hands.

00:08:39.887 --> 00:08:43.890
And use them to see the microscopic world.

00:08:43.891 --> 00:08:46.359
We have done workshop with kids all the way from

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.296
very young kids, for example right here, all the way to adults

00:08:50.297 --> 00:08:53.833
where the goal is to build instruments and learn how to make your own optical

00:08:53.834 --> 00:08:59.605
instruments before you utilize them. This is very similar to what is done in astronomy where

00:08:59.606 --> 00:09:02.441
people build their own telescopes.

00:09:02.442 --> 00:09:07.780
It takes 3-5 minutes to build instruments from each one of these parts.

00:09:07.781 --> 00:09:11.617
And to just simplify the process, we came up with a color scheme

00:09:11.618 --> 00:09:15.755
that allows you to fold these instruments primarily using color instructions.

00:09:15.756 --> 00:09:18.724
So it's kind of like a game, you fold together parts

00:09:18.725 --> 00:09:21.727
based on how these colors come up on each other.

00:09:21.728 --> 00:09:25.097
There are no language or no other instructions that are required

00:09:25.098 --> 00:09:27.533
other than just following this color pattern.

00:09:27.534 --> 00:09:30.036
There are many different of instruments that you can make

00:09:30.037 --> 00:09:35.708
all the way from bright field, dark field, fluorescence, instruments that can do 2 micron resolution,

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:41.681
polarization microscopes. It really depends on how you configure each one of these parts together.

00:09:41.682 --> 00:09:48.421
Here is a class of images that I took from one of the microscopes that I built a couple of months ago.

00:09:48.422 --> 00:09:51.224
There are lots of examples of images of diseases.

00:09:51.225 --> 00:09:53.593
Here is an example of E. coli, or bacillus,

00:09:53.594 --> 00:09:56.929
loa loa, malaria up on the top right there.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:01.200
But one of my favorite images is this of an ant leg

00:10:01.201 --> 00:10:09.775
which a kid while talking to her, asked me a question related to what is the strength in an ant.

00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:11.811
And there are typical answers that you would give,

00:10:11.812 --> 00:10:15.515
but what she was interested in was counting the total number of muscles

00:10:15.516 --> 00:10:18.684
that were there in this ant leg. And as you can see,

00:10:18.685 --> 00:10:23.222
what she identified are these striations of how these muscle structures are arranged

00:10:23.223 --> 00:10:30.763
in the leg of an ant. And yes, sure, maybe in the 1800s and 1900s somebody has

00:10:30.764 --> 00:10:33.766
figured out and documented this. But for her,

00:10:33.767 --> 00:10:35.735
it was a discovery of one.

00:10:35.736 --> 00:10:39.572
It was her curiosity that allowed her to ask that question,

00:10:39.573 --> 00:10:44.677
and using a very simple tool, get an understanding of her own.

00:10:44.678 --> 00:10:48.548
I'm going to now give you a quick demo of what we call a projection microscope,

00:10:48.549 --> 00:10:52.652
which is really designed for sharing images

00:10:52.653 --> 00:10:57.823
with a broader group of people. So I'm going to put in a simple screen right here,

00:10:57.824 --> 00:11:03.563
and just using the exact same instrument, right here

00:11:03.564 --> 00:11:07.200
with just a brighter battery, we're able to project an image

00:11:07.201 --> 00:11:12.672
where a large group of people should be able to share and see these images live.

00:11:12.673 --> 00:11:20.346
What I've put in right here is just a simple squashed head of a fly,

00:11:20.347 --> 00:11:23.015
and you start seeing all these intricate structures.

00:11:23.016 --> 00:11:27.753
This is really the pump that allows flies to feed on sugar, right there.

00:11:27.754 --> 00:11:30.690
And then I'm going to move around to show you something right here,

00:11:30.691 --> 00:11:33.826
which is essentially a filter that's built in,

00:11:33.827 --> 00:11:38.331
right on top of the flies head. It's a bacterial filter.

00:11:38.332 --> 00:11:45.571
You can start seeing all these tiny little bristles which act as chemosensors.

00:11:45.572 --> 00:11:49.375
You can start seeing those tiny little holes in there,

00:11:49.376 --> 00:11:57.483
are essentially how flies breathe. And we move around to find the optics.

00:11:57.484 --> 00:12:00.620
Right here is the optical elements of the fly's eye,

00:12:00.621 --> 00:12:04.924
which is a compound eye, and you can start seeing each one of these little holes,

00:12:04.925 --> 00:12:09.262
they act as essentially breathing tubes.

00:12:09.263 --> 00:12:18.504
Here is the neck, and you can see the entire surface is covered with these tiny, tiny holes,

00:12:18.505 --> 00:12:31.951
which allow flies to breathe just by diffusion of oxygen.

00:12:31.952 --> 00:12:35.888
So what's fascinating about this projection instrument is

00:12:35.889 --> 00:12:40.826
multiple people can share images simultaneously and have a

00:12:40.827 --> 00:12:47.066
conversation about what they're seeing in a live demo.

00:12:47.067 --> 00:12:51.137
One of the reasons that projection microscopes are very useful

00:12:51.138 --> 00:12:58.811
is the fact that it allows us to train microscopists out in the field on remote diagnostics.

00:12:58.812 --> 00:13:06.485
So, for field instruments it's actually important for these instruments to be fairly rugged.

00:13:06.486 --> 00:13:10.523
We had a joke that we would throw these instruments and indeed

00:13:10.524 --> 00:13:16.329
they do survive several stories of a fall. They're extremely rugged instruments

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:18.998
and they're truly designed for field conditions.

00:13:18.999 --> 00:13:26.239
So, I wanted to now end with where are we going.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:27.740
What is the vision that we care about?

00:13:27.741 --> 00:13:31.010
One of the visions that we have is of a world

00:13:31.011 --> 00:13:37.183
where every single kid could carry a microscope in their own pockets, at all times.

00:13:37.184 --> 00:13:42.722
This is very much like people carrying ball-point pens as a tool to write.

00:13:42.723 --> 00:13:46.092
We've been looking at this idea for the last couple of years,

00:13:46.093 --> 00:13:50.630
and sharing and building workshops for how to build these instruments,

00:13:50.631 --> 00:13:56.802
and explore the microcosmic world with everybody around you.

00:13:56.803 --> 00:14:00.840
And one of the factors that we have now identified

00:14:00.841 --> 00:14:07.647
is the idea of building a biology lab manual from the bottom up.

00:14:07.648 --> 00:14:14.887
We would like to recruit almost on the order of 10,000 users from all walks of life for a beta test,

00:14:14.888 --> 00:14:18.891
which essentially involves all the way from teachers, to kids,

00:14:18.892 --> 00:14:23.663
to organizations, citizen-scientists, to traditional scientists

00:14:23.664 --> 00:14:29.802
who are willing to essentially run experiments and share them with an instrument

00:14:29.803 --> 00:14:33.539
like this to a much broader community.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:39.312
I want to close this talk by acknowledging some of the funding sources that we have had,

00:14:39.313 --> 00:14:44.617
and point you to the website which has all the details for how to build these instruments,

00:14:44.618 --> 00:14:51.657
the technical details, and at the same time, a place to sign up if you want to be one of those beta users.

00:14:51.658 --> 00:15:19.219
Thank you so much for you time.

